1
00:00:00,046 --> 00:00:05,796
Train a vision model on images and a language model on text, and something strange happens inside them.

2
00:00:06,254 --> 00:00:08,228
Their internal vectors look nothing alike.

3
00:00:08,526 --> 00:00:11,940
Different dimensions, different scales, different directions entirely.

4
00:00:12,366 --> 00:00:13,476
But ask a different question.

5
00:00:13,966 --> 00:00:17,028
Not where is each point, but which points sit near which other points.

6
00:00:18,254 --> 00:00:23,172
Suddenly the two models agree, and the bigger and better these models get, the more they agree.

7
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:27,332
That's the observation a new paper from Jan LeCun's group turns into a theorem.

8
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:30,980
To see why, start with a picture that's about 2,000 years old.

9
00:00:31,566 --> 00:00:35,812
Plato imagined prisoners in a cave who could only see shadows cast on a wall.

10
00:00:36,142 --> 00:00:37,444
Here's the machine learning version.

11
00:00:37,774 --> 00:00:40,080
There's some hidden state of the world, call it Z.

12
00:00:40,654 --> 00:00:43,396
Every piece of data you ever collect is a shadow of it.

13
00:00:43,726 --> 00:00:46,724
A camera applies one function to Z and gives you pixels.

14
00:00:47,054 --> 00:00:49,828
A microphone applies another and gives you a waveform.

15
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:53,060
A person applies a third and gives you a sentence.

16
00:00:53,646 --> 00:00:55,620
Each one adds a little noise on top.

17
00:00:56,078 --> 00:00:57,348
That's all this equation says.

18
00:00:57,742 --> 00:01:01,380
Your observation is some function of the hidden reality, plus junk.

19
00:01:01,966 --> 00:01:03,268
And notice what it implies.

20
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:06,276
Every single shadow throws information away.

21
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:09,988
From one shadow alone, many different objects could have cast it.

22
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:12,676
But here's the move that makes the whole thing work.

23
00:01:13,262 --> 00:01:15,780
Each modality throws away different information.

24
00:01:16,238 --> 00:01:20,004
Start with every possible world that could have produced the image you saw.

25
00:01:20,366 --> 00:01:22,116
That's an enormous cloud of candidates.

26
00:01:22,606 --> 00:01:25,636
Now demand that the same world also explain the caption.

27
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:27,396
Most of the candidates die.

28
00:01:27,854 --> 00:01:29,732
Demand it also explain the audio.

29
00:01:30,094 --> 00:01:31,748
Most of what's left dies too.

30
00:01:32,142 --> 00:01:38,756
The constraints intersect, and the set of worlds consistent with all of your senses at once shrinks toward a single answer.

31
00:01:40,014 --> 00:01:44,260
That shrinking is the entire engine behind the platonic representation hypothesis.

32
00:01:44,654 --> 00:01:49,572
Now, a careful caveat, and it's the one that makes the claim provable instead of just poetic.

33
00:01:50,094 --> 00:01:51,760
You can never recover Z exactly.

34
00:01:52,014 --> 00:01:57,924
If I rotate my entire representation or scale it or relabel the axes, nothing observable changes.

35
00:01:58,414 --> 00:01:59,812
The model is just as good.

36
00:02:00,238 --> 00:02:04,612
So recovering reality can only ever mean recovering it up to a reversible relabeling.

37
00:02:04,942 --> 00:02:06,640
The shape of the space is the real thing.

38
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:08,932
The coordinates are arbitrary bookkeeping.

39
00:02:09,326 --> 00:02:16,004
What's forbidden is a squash that merges two distinct things into one point because that destroys information and can't be undone.

40
00:02:16,622 --> 00:02:18,372
This is what the equation on screen means.

41
00:02:19,022 --> 00:02:23,716
The learned features are the hidden state, viewed through some invertible change of coordinates.

42
00:02:24,238 --> 00:02:27,684
So how do you actually train a model to climb toward that hidden state?

43
00:02:28,206 --> 00:02:30,372
The natural idea is prediction.

44
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:33,220
Show the model the present, ask it for the future.

45
00:02:33,646 --> 00:02:35,684
Here's a ball rolling toward a fork in the track.

46
00:02:36,238 --> 00:02:39,012
The genuinely uncertain part is which branch it takes.

47
00:02:39,534 --> 00:02:45,956
If you train the model to predict the next frame of raw pixels with a squared error loss, there's a mathematical fact waiting for you.

48
00:02:46,702 --> 00:02:50,180
The loss is minimized by the average over all possible futures.

49
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:52,868
Not one future, the mean of all of them.

50
00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:58,084
So the optimal model paints a ghost of the ball on every branch at once, and you get a smear.

51
00:02:58,446 --> 00:03:08,612
Worse, the model has to spend its capacity modeling leaf textures and sensor noise and the exact value of every pixel, detail that is unpredictable in principle and useless in practice.

52
00:03:09,102 --> 00:03:10,052
Now change one thing.

53
00:03:10,414 --> 00:03:12,324
Don't predict the future observation.

54
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:14,436
Predict the future representation.

55
00:03:14,894 --> 00:03:17,860
Push all ten possible futures through the encoder first.

56
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:23,908
The encoder is allowed to discard which branch the ball took, because that's exactly the part it cannot know.

57
00:03:24,270 --> 00:03:29,412
And once it does, all ten futures land on essentially the same point in representation space.

58
00:03:29,902 --> 00:03:31,588
There's nothing left to average over.

59
00:03:31,982 --> 00:03:33,444
So the predictor can be sharp.

60
00:03:34,286 --> 00:03:40,740
Same loss function, same squared distance, but measured after the unpredictable detail has already been thrown away.

61
00:03:41,102 --> 00:03:44,800
Except there's an obvious cheat, and the optimizer will find it immediately.

62
00:03:45,134 --> 00:03:49,956
If the encoder maps every input to the same constant vector, prediction becomes perfect.

63
00:03:50,862 --> 00:03:51,744
Zero loss.

64
00:03:52,110 --> 00:03:55,236
And the representation carries no information whatsoever.

65
00:03:55,630 --> 00:03:56,420
This is collapse.

66
00:03:57,006 --> 00:04:02,052
And on the loss surface, it looks like a funnel with everything sliding into a single point at the bottom.

67
00:04:02,478 --> 00:04:05,124
So you add a second term that pushes in the opposite direction.

68
00:04:05,614 --> 00:04:07,264
Keep the representation spread out.

69
00:04:07,502 --> 00:04:08,704
Keep its entropy high.

70
00:04:08,864 --> 00:04:09,764
Keep it informative.

71
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:12,772
Think of it as inflating a balloon inside the funnel.

72
00:04:13,134 --> 00:04:13,956
Two forces.

73
00:04:14,446 --> 00:04:15,844
Make the features predictable.

74
00:04:16,270 --> 00:04:17,540
Don't let them go empty.

75
00:04:17,902 --> 00:04:22,916
That's the entire objective on screen, and the paper's claim is that these two terms alone are enough.

76
00:04:23,406 --> 00:04:26,692
Here's the argument, and it's beautifully simple once you see the shape of it.

77
00:04:27,182 --> 00:04:29,444
Consider the space of all possible encoders.

78
00:04:29,742 --> 00:04:36,452
The predictability term cuts away every encoder that keeps noise, because noise by definition can't be predicted from the past.

79
00:04:37,134 --> 00:04:38,404
Keeping it only costs you.

80
00:04:38,798 --> 00:04:40,804
The entropy term cuts from the other side.

81
00:04:41,166 --> 00:04:48,544
It kills every encoder that throws away structure which could have been predicted, because discarding real information needlessly shrinks the spread.

82
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:53,892
Squeeze from both sides, and what survives is exactly the predictable part of the world and nothing else.

83
00:04:54,446 --> 00:04:56,868
That predictable part is the hidden state Z.

84
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:08,004
Not Z in any particular coordinate system, the surviving solutions form a family of rotations and rescalings of one shared shape, which is precisely the identifiability we agreed to settle for.

85
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:10,948
Now we can ask the question everyone actually cares about.

86
00:05:11,342 --> 00:05:15,652
If all data are shadows of the same reality, does it matter which shadow you train on?

87
00:05:16,046 --> 00:05:18,628
It matters enormously, and the reason is bandwidth.

88
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:22,372
Text is a beautiful signal about the world, but it is a narrow one.

89
00:05:22,830 --> 00:05:27,076
Every sentence has already been compressed by a human who decided what was worth saying.

90
00:05:27,502 --> 00:05:32,196
A video stream or a sensor feed is a far wider channel into the same reservoir.

91
00:05:32,654 --> 00:05:36,708
Orders of magnitude, more constraints per second on what the world could be.

92
00:05:37,134 --> 00:05:42,308
Feed the narrow pipe into the identifiability argument and you get a hidden state recovered with holes in it.

93
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:45,764
Whole regions of reality that language simply never pinned down.

94
00:05:46,318 --> 00:05:48,464
Feed the wide pipe and the reconstruction fills in.

95
00:05:48,718 --> 00:05:51,300
And now the part that turns a hypothesis into a tool.

96
00:05:51,662 --> 00:05:58,340
If convergence toward one shared representation is a theorem rather than an accident, you don't have to wait for scale to deliver it.

97
00:05:58,542 --> 00:05:59,812
You can push on it directly.

98
00:06:00,174 --> 00:06:02,660
Watch what happens as capacity and data grow.

99
00:06:03,278 --> 00:06:09,892
The vision constellation and the audio constellation and the text constellation all drift toward the same shape on their own.

100
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:17,380
Now add an explicit force, a term that pulls matching points across modalities together, like springs between the clouds.

101
00:06:17,934 --> 00:06:21,892
They snap into alignment far faster than scale alone would manage.

102
00:06:22,638 --> 00:06:29,188
Cross-modal alignment stops being a happy side effect and becomes a deliberate lever on how quickly you approach the ideal.

103
00:06:29,614 --> 00:06:31,876
This also gives you something you can actually test.

104
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:39,588
If two encoders really converge to the same shape, a probe trained on one modality's features should work on another's without retraining.

105
00:06:40,078 --> 00:06:43,264
Train the slicing plane using only vision features.

106
00:06:43,598 --> 00:06:45,024
Hand it audio features.

107
00:06:45,294 --> 00:06:49,732
It still separates them correctly because it's cutting through the same underlying geometry.

108
00:06:50,094 --> 00:06:56,772
It also explains why structure nobody ever labeled, like depth, shows up cleanly inside self supervised visual features.

109
00:06:57,454 --> 00:07:04,516
Depth was in the hidden state all along, and the objective had no reason to discard it, which leaves you with a single dial to turn.

110
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:10,944
The width of the channel you let your model look through determines how much of the reality it can ever pin down.

